. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
.  
.         * Please add a verbal description of your model here
.         
. /*      
> We construct the outcome variable, gender_equality, by standardizing response
> s (mean = 0, standard deviation = 1) to survey question plh0308_v2, which ask
> s respondents the extent to which they agree with the statement: "Best if man
>  and woman work the same amount so they can share the responsibility." Higher
>  values of gender_equality thus indicate stronger support for gender equality
>  in labor participation.
> 
> Building on the approach used in Hypothesis 1, we estimate a linear regressio
> n of the gender_equality outcome on an indicator for having a daughter. In ad
> dition to including the respondent's gender as a control, we introduce an int
> eraction term between daughter and male. The coefficient of interest is that 
> of the interaction term. As in the specification for Hypothesis 1, we control
>  for the respondent's age, income, years of education, nationality, marital s
> tatus, survey year, and the age of the child. The interaction coefficient cap
> tures the differential effect of having a daughter (versus a son) on support 
> for gender equality among female respondents relative to male respondents. As
>  before, the outcome is standardized, so that the effect is expressed in stan
> dard deviation units.
> 
> The sample is restricted to individuals with exactly one child. Since the out
> come variable is only available in the 2018 wave, this restriction ensures th
> at there is a single observation per respondent. To minimize child's gender s
> election, we exclude homosexuals.
> */
.         
.         
.         global controls_h4 "years_edu   age age_child married  ln_income i.sy
> ear i.nationality"

.         
.         
.         *h4 -> only 2018
.         reg gender_equality i.daughter##i.male $controls_h4  if sample==1 , r
> obust      
note: 2018.syear omitted because of collinearity.

